{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And one who strikes his father.</b> And if you ask: from where is its warning? For the verse did not punish unless it warned — fine, for striking we derive from ״the prince among your people you shall not curse,״ and it is written ״you shall not curse the deaf,״ which warns concerning the greatest in Israel and the smallest in Israel, to tell you that whatever is between them falls under ״you shall not curse״ — but the warning for striking, from where is it derived? And one answers: from ״he shall not add to strike him,״ which is about one who wounds his fellow for less than the value of a perutah, for if it were for the value of a perutah, he would stand liable monetarily, since the halakhah follows Rabbi Meir who said that wherever there is both monetary liability and lashes, he pays money but is not lashed — but for less than the value of a perutah, where there is no monetary liability, he is lashed on account of ״he shall not add,״ and his father is no less than others. And if you ask: since this is a warning for one who strikes his father, how can they administer lashes for this negative commandment, for it is a negative commandment given as a warning for the death penalty of the court, and one is not lashed for it? And one answers: two verses are written — ״לא יוסיף״ and ״ופן יוסיף״. And further one can answer: since the main thrust of the verse concerns one who wounds his fellow, this is not called a negative commandment given as a warning for the death penalty of the court. And if you ask: from where do we know that this striker, without this, is liable? For fine, for cursing we derive it from the fact that the verse wrote ״his father and his mother,״ attaching the curse to the father, and in another place it is written ״his father and his mother he cursed,״ which attached the curse to the mother — but striking, from where? And one answers: we derive it from what is written in the section אמור אל הכהנים: ״and one who strikes a man shall be put to death,״ and Rashi explained in ״one who strikes a man״ — just as ״one who strikes an animal shall pay for it,״ just as striking an animal means he did not kill, so too striking a man means he did not kill him, and the Merciful One says ״shall be put to death״ — thus you learn that the verse speaks of one who strikes his father. And one cannot say it speaks of striking another and he pays money, as in ״and its owner too shall be put to death,״ for it is written just before it: ״as one gives a blemish in his fellow, so shall be given in him,״ which is money — thus ״shall be put to death״ means literally death, and it wants to say this about one who strikes his father."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:48.419422121Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}